cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this model or its outputs, please cite it as below. The numbers
  of a release are those of its full run (python run_all.py --profile full),
  committed under outputs/ and locked by tests/test_regression_model.py
  (tests/regression_lock.json).
type: software
title: >-
  New Zealand new residential floor area and embodied carbon, 2026–2050:
  projection, planetary-boundary layer (AESA) and pathways
version: 4.10.2
date-released: "2026-11-23"
license: MIT
authors:
  - family-names: Christoforatos
    given-names: Gerasimos
    affiliation: "University of Waikato, New Zealand"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-9351-7497"
repository-code: "https://github.com/GerasimosChristoforatos/Pathway-Paper"
abstract: >-
  A bottom-up, demand-driven projection of the new residential floor area New
  Zealand builds in 2026–2050 and its embodied carbon (materials and
  construction A1–A5, soil carbon loss, maintenance B2/B4, and the emissions
  committed after 2050), with Monte Carlo and Sobol uncertainty analysis,
  validation against the census record, and an absolute environmental
  sustainability assessment (AESA) of climate change under the acquired-rights
  and ability-to-pay allocation principles. Ten levers, grouped by the PAS 2080
  carbon reduction hierarchy (Avoid L1–L5, Switch L6–L8, Improve L9–L10), are
  set at Moderate and Ambitious levels and combined into the Moderate and
  Ambitious Pathways, with exact Shapley attribution, a back-cast and a pathway
  Monte Carlo. The Theoretical Limit sets every lever at the best value
  observed internationally or in New Zealand, or at a documented scenario
  bound; a logical bound only where physically attainable. It is a thought
  experiment, not a pathway.
keywords:
  - embodied carbon
  - residential construction
  - floor area projection
  - absolute environmental sustainability assessment
  - planetary boundaries
  - New Zealand
